    return describe.Tool { name: "ml-predict", version: "0.1.0", summary: "Apply a fitted model to a CSV and print what a model file requires of its caller, with the refusals of a tool an agent can read. The prediction half of ml, built from the same sources: it reads a model file written by ml fit and writes nothing but its answer (no file, no log, no thread). predict applies the stored schema, steps and model to a CSV (a file, or standard input for the operand - or none; --proba adds the probability of a classification model, --distance the squared distance of a row to its cluster centre for a kmeans model) and prints the same bytes as ml predict. describe prints, as data, what the model file needs from a caller: the model and its task, the plan, the columns by name and type, the categorical vocabularies, the missing-value markers, the reader limits, the classes and the columns of an answer. Every refusal is a rule tag with its hint. Verbs: predict FILE or predict - (--model-file; --proba, --distance, --drop, --limit, --from, --max-rows, --max-columns, --max-cells, --max-line-bytes, --max-bytes), describe (--model-file; --limit, --max-bytes). The first operand is the verb, the second the CSV file.", usage: "predict: ml-predict [--root DIR] --model-file MODEL [--proba] [--distance] [--drop NAMES] [--limit N] [--from N] [--max-rows N] [--max-columns N] [--max-cells N] [--max-line-bytes N] [--max-bytes N] [--format json|text] predict FILE|-\ndescribe: ml-predict [--root DIR] --model-file MODEL [--limit N] [--max-bytes N] [--format json|text] describe\n\nFILE: a CSV with a header row, comma separated, RFC 4180 quoting, a byte order mark, CRLF and blank lines read. Cells are integers, decimals or floating-point numbers; nan and inf are refused; an empty cell is a missing value for the models that take one. Paths are relative to --root when given. The operand - or no operand is standard input (--root does not apply to it).", output: "document", schema: "ml.v1", flags: predict_flag_table(), operands: "VERB|none|1|1|predict or describe;FILE|path-read|0|1|the CSV file to read (predict: - or no operand is standard input; describe takes none), a path relative to --root when given (absolute paths, .. and symbolic links are refused), whose first row is the header", rules: rules(), extra_rules: extra(), limits: predict_limits(), reversibility: "reversible-cheap", stdin: "predict reads the CSV from standard input when FILE is - or absent", guarantees: "deterministic;idempotent" };
